      "cml:premiseText": "Judge/Magistrate orders",
      "cml:conclusionText": "Offender must permit procedure"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "26(4) Crimes Amendment (Forensic Procedures) Act 2001",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001#sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001.pdf, p.26",
      "values:originalText": "(4) An offender ordered to permit the carrying out of a forensic\nprocedure is guilty of an offence if the offender, without\nreasonable excuse, refuses or fails to permit the forensic procedure\nto be carried out.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote:\nA defendant bears the evidential burden in relation to the exception of\nreasonable excuse—see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code.",
      "skos:definition": "(4) An offender ordered to permit the carrying out of a forensic\nprocedure is guilty of an offence if the offender, without\nreasonable excuse, refuses or fails to permit the forensic procedure\nto be carried out.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote:\nA defendant bears the evidential burden in relation to the exception of\nreasonable excuse—see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-logic-deontic-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-logic-deontic-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-logic-linearlogic-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-logic-alleninterval-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "35b7a61028b9c346aa5b74ff3f8ec53b4213b91b405c5b856906294d2ab8d605",
      "skos:note": "Authorized applicant may apply for order to take fingerprints of a child or incapable person. Refusal to permit forensic procedure without reasonable excuse is an offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001#sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "26(4) Crimes Amendment (Forensic Procedures) Act 2001",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 26,
      "values:originalText": "(4) An offender ordered to permit the carrying out of a forensic\nprocedure is guilty of an offence if the offender, without\nreasonable excuse, refuses or fails to permit the forensic procedure\nto be carried out.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 12 months.\nNote:\nA defendant bears the evidential burden in relation to the exception of\nreasonable excuse—see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-26-2-ss-4-norm",
