    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "32(2) Crimes Amendment (Forensic Procedures) Act 2001",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001#sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001.pdf, p.32",
      "values:originalText": "(2) A magistrate may order that forensic material taken or information\nobtained from carrying out a forensic procedure on a volunteer\nwho withdraws consent, or parent or guardian of a volunteer who\nwithdraws consent, as the case may be, to the retention of the\nmaterial be retained if the magistrate is satisfied that:\n(a) during an investigation into the commission of a serious\noffence, material reasonably believed to be from the body of\na person who committed the offence had been found:\n(i) at the scene of the offence; or\n(ii) on the victim of the offence or anything reasonably\nbelieved to have been worn or carried by the victim\nwhen the offence was committed; or\n(iii) on the volunteer or anything reasonably believed to\nhave been worn or carried by the volunteer at the scene\nof the offence or when the offence was committed; or\n(iv) on an object or person reasonably believed to have been\nassociated with the commission of the offence; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that information\nobtained from analysis of the forensic material taken from\nthe volunteer is likely to produce evidence of probative value\nin relation to the serious offence being investigated; and\n(c) the retention of the forensic material taken from the volunteer\nis justified in all the circumstances.",
      "skos:definition": "(2) A magistrate may order that forensic material taken or information\nobtained from carrying out a forensic procedure on a volunteer\nwho withdraws consent, or parent or guardian of a volunteer who\nwithdraws consent, as the case may be, to the retention of the\nmaterial be retained if the magistrate is satisfied that:\n(a) during an investigation into the commission of a serious\noffence, material reasonably believed to be from the body of\na person who committed the offence had been found:\n(i) at the scene of the offence; or\n(ii) on the victim of the offence or anything reasonably\nbelieved to have been worn or carried by the victim\nwhen the offence was committed; or\n(iii) on the volunteer or anything reasonably believed to\nhave been worn or carried by the volunteer at the scene\nof the offence or when the offence was committed; or\n(iv) on an object or person reasonably believed to have been\nassociated with the commission of the offence; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that information\nobtained from analysis of the forensic material taken from\nthe volunteer is likely to produce evidence of probative value\nin relation to the serious offence being investigated; and\n(c) the retention of the forensic material taken from the volunteer\nis justified in all the circumstances.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-logic-deontic-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-logic-deontic-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-logic-deontic-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-logic-linearlogic-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "fc4d58a94db31610665f3ab900f0c7ed0c64fc0a8b179b97d729d51a6be8fe5d",
      "skos:note": "Magistrate may order retention of forensic material under certain conditions. Magistrate must consider certain factors when determining whether to make an order under this section."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001#sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "32(2) Crimes Amendment (Forensic Procedures) Act 2001",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 32,
      "values:originalText": "(2) A magistrate may order that forensic material taken or information\nobtained from carrying out a forensic procedure on a volunteer\nwho withdraws consent, or parent or guardian of a volunteer who\nwithdraws consent, as the case may be, to the retention of the\nmaterial be retained if the magistrate is satisfied that:\n(a) during an investigation into the commission of a serious\noffence, material reasonably believed to be from the body of\na person who committed the offence had been found:\n(i) at the scene of the offence; or\n(ii) on the victim of the offence or anything reasonably\nbelieved to have been worn or carried by the victim\nwhen the offence was committed; or\n(iii) on the volunteer or anything reasonably believed to\nhave been worn or carried by the volunteer at the scene\nof the offence or when the offence was committed; or\n(iv) on an object or person reasonably believed to have been\nassociated with the commission of the offence; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that information\nobtained from analysis of the forensic material taken from\nthe volunteer is likely to produce evidence of probative value\nin relation to the serious offence being investigated; and\n(c) the retention of the forensic material taken from the volunteer\nis justified in all the circumstances."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-amendment-forensic-procedures-act-2001-sch-1-sec-32-2-ss-2-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
